Report a date output format that strftime() can't handle.
date ignored the result of strftime(), so when it failed, date printed
an empty line and exited with status 0. strftime() fails when the output
doesn't fit in date's 1 KiB buffer, and the Microsoft C runtime also
fails on any conversion it doesn't support, such as %F or %Q.

Append a space to the format, so that a successful result is never
empty, check for a zero length, and remove the space again. On failure
report an error and exit with status 1.

---
 coreutils/date.c |    9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/coreutils/date.c
+++ b/coreutils/date.c
@@ -378,8 +378,13 @@
 		/* With no format string, just print a blank line */
 		date_buf[0] = '\0';
 	} else {
-		/* Generate output string */
-		strftime(date_buf, COMMON_BUFSIZE, fmt_dt2str, &tm_time);
+		/* Generate output string. The added space, removed below,
+		 * makes it nonempty, so a zero length means failure */
+		size_t len = strftime(date_buf, COMMON_BUFSIZE,
+				xasprintf("%s ", fmt_dt2str), &tm_time);
+		if (len == 0)
+			bb_simple_error_msg_and_die("invalid format, or output too long");
+		date_buf[len - 1] = '\0';
 	}
 	puts(date_buf);
 
